Pending-channel waits: 50 us polls after the 500 us yield window, 1 ms after 2 s.
Residual of zortos293/Eglantine acb9c18 (#446). Main already yields for the first 500 us since the
last progress and then waits with state.queue.PopWaitFor(next, 250 us), which a new command wakes
early. The wait is now 50 us (Ocarina of Time waits here ~330 times a second and its present waits
for the work behind the semaphore; upstream desktop saw ~17 ms a frame fall to ~1 ms), and a channel
still waiting 2 s after its last progress (menus, loading) polls every 1 ms so it does not spin at
50 us forever. PopWaitFor is kept, not a sleep_for. No prctl/timer slack: this build targets
PS5/FreeBSD. Timer granularity there is not measured.
--- a/src/video_core/gpu_thread.cpp
+++ b/src/video_core/gpu_thread.cpp
@@ -83,15 +83,20 @@
                     // The CPU usually releases the semaphore soon after: poll for half a
                     // millisecond before sleeping, or every wait also waits out a host sleep.
                     Profile::Scope scope{Profile::Zone::PendingSleep};
-                    if (std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - last_progress <
-                        std::chrono::microseconds{500}) {
+                    const auto waited = std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - last_progress;
+                    if (waited < std::chrono::microseconds{500}) {
                         std::this_thread::yield();
                         continue;
                     }
                     // Then sleep until the CPU submits more work, polling the semaphores every
-                    // quarter millisecond (a release is a plain guest memory write that wakes
-                    // nothing). A fixed 1 ms sleep let new submissions wait out the rest of it.
-                    if (!state.queue.PopWaitFor(next, std::chrono::microseconds{250})) {
+                    // 50 us (a release is a plain guest memory write that wakes nothing; Ocarina
+                    // of Time waits here ~330 times a second and its present waits for the work
+                    // behind them). A channel still waiting after 2 s (menus, loading) goes back
+                    // to 1 ms. A fixed sleep let new submissions wait out the rest of it.
+                    const auto poll = waited < std::chrono::seconds{2}
+                                          ? std::chrono::microseconds{50}
+                                          : std::chrono::microseconds{1000};
+                    if (!state.queue.PopWaitFor(next, poll)) {
                         continue;
                     }
                 }
